      <div>
        <h2>What happens during the first appointment?</h2>
        <p>Your first appointment is an opportunity to understand what you are experiencing and discuss your goals.</p>
        <p>Your psychiatric provider may ask about your mental-health history, current symptoms, medications, sleep, mood, energy, concentration, relationships, work or school, and other factors that may be relevant.</p>
        <p>The appointment is also a conversation. You should have time to ask questions and discuss your concerns.</p>
      </div>
      {% include "partials/first-appointment-pill.njk" %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <h2>What happens after the appointment?</h2>
    <p>Depending on your treatment plan, follow-up care may include medication management, additional evaluation, psychotherapy, behavioral strategies, or coordination with other members of your care team.</p>
    <p>If medication is prescribed, your provider will discuss the treatment plan and follow-up needs with you.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band lp-band--gold">
    <h2>Is telehealth right for everyone?</h2>
    <p>Telehealth can be a convenient option, but it is not the right setting for every situation.</p>
    <p>If you are experiencing an emergency or are in immediate danger, call 911 or go to the nearest emergency department. If you are experiencing a mental-health crisis, call or text 988.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <h2>A note for Washington patients</h2>
    <p>You must be physically located in a state where your provider is authorized to provide telehealth care at the time of your appointment. For Meaningful Psychiatry's Washington telehealth services, patients should be in Washington during the visit.</p>
  </section>

